# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""tools/manager_bot_access_repair_selftest.py -- offline selftest for the
final, narrow "ManagerBot access restore" patch (manager_bot.py, single file).

Business problem fixed: a manager fully deleted via AdminBot and reconnected
did not regain ManagerBot access. Root cause: main.py's
_manager_delete_full_core deletes access_targets and the managers row but
NEVER touches access_users -- so access_users.is_enabled=1 survives, and the
active /start handler's `if _access_allowed(uid): ... return` short-circuit
(manager_bot.py, unchanged by this patch) made the repair path unreachable for
exactly that state.

WHY THIS CONTRACT IS THIS NARROW (read before touching anything in this
file): an earlier version of `_mbaccess_restore_access` ALSO created a
missing access_users row. Independent review proved that version caused a
regression of the exact card-burst defect class this whole effort exists to
avoid: creating access_users+access_targets with no manager_bot_access row let
the pre-existing init_schema M2.6B backfill create one on the next ManagerBot
restart with event_cutoff_id=0 and auto_granted=0 -- a state M2.6E
deliberately never repairs -- making the manager's entire historical
manager_bot_events backlog deliverable (proven empirically, 50/50 events).

The final helper repairs ONLY a missing access_targets row, and ONLY when
BOTH access_users AND a manager_bot_access row for this exact (uid,
manager_key) already exist -- i.e. only the exact state
_manager_delete_full_core leaves behind. It never creates access_users or
manager_bot_access, never calls _reconcile_reconnected_manager or
_auto_grant_manager_bot_access, and never reads/writes
manager_bot_events / manager_bot_sent / manager_lead_cards / event_cutoff_id.
Every precondition is re-verified inside one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction
immediately before the INSERT (X4/X5/X6/X9 below prove each guard actually
matters; R-series "race" checks prove the transactional re-check, not just
the outer one, is what closes the TOCTOU window).

A uid with no access_users row, or no manager_bot_access row for this key
(brand-new manager, first /start, or reconnect under a NEW manager_key) gets
"not_applicable" and falls through, unchanged, to the pre-existing
_reconcile_reconnected_manager / _auto_grant_manager_bot_access path -- the
only place that computes event_cutoff_id from manager_bot_events, and which
remains completely unmodified (verified by X9's backfill-burst reproduction:
removing the very guard that routes this case away is what brings the burst
back).

Techniques (matching the project's own tools/*_selftest.py conventions):
  * manager_bot.py is NOT importable (Telethon/env side effects at import
    time) -> the relevant functions are AST-extracted (last top-level def
    wins, matching the project's override-stack convention) and exec'd into a
    namespace seeded with fakes for Telethon/env/log boundaries and, crucially,
    fakes for `_reconcile_reconnected_manager` / `_auto_grant_manager_bot_access`
    that RECORD whether they were ever called -- the whole point of this patch
    is that the new restore path must never touch them, while the pre-existing
    fallback path (when the new helper reports "not_applicable") must still
    reach them exactly as before.
  * mutation / negative-control proofs: apply a small text mutation to the
    extracted source and prove the NAMED check now fails, for the RIGHT
    reason (positive behavioral assertions, never "the mutant crashed").
  * the M2.6B backfill and M2.6E cutoff-repair SQL used in the BLOCKER
    regression proof are AST-extracted (as literal string constants) from the
    real init_schema in manager_bot.py, not retyped -- so this test can never
    silently drift from the real migration the way a hand copy could.
  * a self-guard AST-scans THIS file for any `check(...)` call whose condition
    is a literal that cannot be False -- proven correct against 8 forms by a
    dedicated probe (ported from the prior round's audited fix).

Pure/offline: no network, no Telegram, no production DB, no spend.

    python tools\\manager_bot_access_repair_selftest.py
"""
